Hi there, I've been learning about `mirai` and wanted to ask if there are any recommendations on how to use it from within a package (sort of like [this futureverse vignette](https://future.futureverse.org/articles/future-7-for-package-developers.html)). Apologies if this exists and I just missed it, but I was left with some lingering questions after looking over the pkgdown site and the GitHub issues.

Ideally I'd like my main function to provide a `.cores` argument that handles all the parallelization details for my users when set to 2 or more, but maybe that's inconsistent with the design of `mirai`.

* Should I the developer set up the daemons or should I leave that to the user?
* How should I invoke `mirai` from within my package? I'm not sure if replacing all instances of `lapply()` with `mirai_map()` would work. Ideally I wouldn't need to spawn any additional R processes if the user doesn't want parallelization. I'd prefer to find some way to use `lapply()` by default, but use `mirai_map()` when the user sets `.cores ` to 2 or more. I guess I could try a custom map function like this that `lapply()`s if `.cores == 1` and `mirai_map()`s otherwise, but this seems pretty hacky.

```{r}
my_map = function(.x, .f, ..., .cores = 1L) {
  if (.cores == 1) {
    lapply(.x, .f, ...)
  } else {
    mirai_map(.x, .f, ..., 
              .args = list(other_pkg_fn1 = f1, 
                           other_pkg_fn2 = f2))[]
  }
}
```

* The other option here might be just to export a second version of my function that's identical but uses `mirai_map()`. The downside here is code duplication of course. 
* What's the best way to make other functions from my package available on the daemons? Would I have to run `everywhere()` for each one or could I do `everywhere(library(mypackage))`? The latter seems dangerous if my package is handling the daemon setup.
* My package includes some C++ functions that I run with Rcpp. Are there any special considerations I should be aware of on that front?

If it's helpful, my package is [here](https://github.com/andrewGhazi/fsummary).

Apologies if these are naive questions, I'm still internalizing the design of `mirai`. It seems like really excellent work, the performance in my tests so far is amazing.

